            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the French for accordion, then the French for to tune."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: la chorale",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "la chorale — \"choir\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Je chante dans une chorale depuis trois ans. I have been singing in a choir for three years."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "An amateur choir; le chœur is a choir too, also in a church."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "la chorale comes from choral, built on Medieval Latin chorālis, from Latin chorus \"a dance, a choir\", a borrowing from Greek khorós."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for telling, explaining and describing at B1."
            }
          ]
        },
